CONSULTATIONS.append(dict(consultation_id=con_ids[10], doctor_id=doc5_id, patient_id=pat4_id, appointment_id=apt_ids[10], transcription='''[0:00] DOCTOR: Would you mind by starting with what brought you in today?\n[0:15] PATIENT: Yeah, so I'm just coming in with my son. He's six years old and yeah, just over the last few days he's not been himself and he's been having this stomach ache. Uh, so it started around like I would say 3 days ago and then he's also having vomiting for the last two days, he's vomited in total six times over the last two days and then yesterday he also developed a fever as well. I managed to measure it and it was 38.3 degrees Celsius.\n[0:30] DOCTOR: OK, I see. So I just so that I understand the timeline, so he started to have abdominal pain about 3 days ago, started with having vomiting and diarrhea two days ago and you checked his temperature, he had a fever as of yesterday?\n[0:45] PATIENT: Yeah.\n[1:00] DOCTOR: OK, and uh, was there anything that that he ate that was different over over these last few days?\n[1:15] PATIENT: No, we've been strictly home food during like a pandemic like no, no real changes.\n[1:30] DOCTOR: OK, and what's his kind of behavior been like over these last couple of days?\n[1:45] PATIENT: Yeah, so at first with the stomach pain he was a bit just irritable and just not himself, but especially yesterday with the fever just been very quiet and just kind of sleeping pretty much the whole day.\n[2:00] DOCTOR: OK, I see and uh, could you describe the vomit?\n[2:15] PATIENT: Yes, so vomit like, it started two days ago. The first day it was just like he puked up the things that he had eaten, but yesterday it was it was mainly just like uh just yellowish material, I couldn't really tell if it was food or not.\n[2:30] DOCTOR: OK, have you noticed any bile or blood in the vomit?\n[2:45] PATIENT: Uh no I didn't notice any blood and I didn't notice any green material. It was mainly just like like normal vomit, like yellowish color.\n[3:00] DOCTOR: OK, I see and, has the what was the vomiting triggered by anything that you're aware of, like eating or drinking?\n[3:15] PATIENT: Uhm? Yeah, two days ago it was it was after after lunch when he first vomited. But after that it's just been kind of random.\n[3:30] DOCTOR: I see OK, and, uh, and with regards to his diarrhea, has there been any any blood or or any color changes?\n[3:45] PATIENT: No, no, I didn't notice any blood at all. It's definitely just very very watery and he's had to go probably even just in the last day, probably around like six or seven times.\n[4:00] DOCTOR: OK, um, and he's vomited how many times?\n[4:15] PATIENT: So in total I can count 6 times.\n[4:30] DOCTOR: OK, and, has he been complaining of any headaches at all?\n[4:45] PATIENT: No, not that I know.\n[5:00] DOCTOR: No. OK, and how about any ear pain or or eye pain?\n[5:15] PATIENT: No, not that he's mentioned to me.\n[5:30] DOCTOR: OK, and any eye redness?\n[5:45] PATIENT: Nope.\n[6:00] DOCTOR: OK, and as he had a runny nose?\n[6:15] PATIENT: No, not really.\n[6:30] DOCTOR: OK and has he had a sore throat or any pain with swallowing?\n[6:45] PATIENT: No.\n[7:00] DOCTOR: OK, have you had any loss of taste or loss of smell?\n[7:15] PATIENT: Uh no, not that he has mentioned to me now.\n[7:30] DOCTOR: OK, and has he had a cough?\n[7:45] PATIENT: Uh, no, not really.\n[8:00] DOCTOR: OK, and how about any shortness of breath or wheezing?\n[8:15] PATIENT: No.\n[8:30] DOCTOR: OK, and has he had any any pain anywhere? Any chest pain?\n[8:45] PATIENT: Um, he did, he did point to his stomach like it seems like he was having with all the vomiting like, he was having some stomach pain as well.\n[9:00] DOCTOR: OK, is there any specific location that that he's feeling the pain?\n[9:15] PATIENT: Well, it's hard to say. He was just pointing at the center of his belly like, I don't know if it's like, I tried pressing on it and he didn't feel any pain then, but I think it's just kind of all over his stomach.\n[9:30] DOCTOR: OK, and has he had any urinary problems like painful voiding or been peeing more often?\n[9:45] PATIENT: No, that's been OK.\n[10:00] DOCTOR: Have you noticed any rashes or skin changes?\n[10:15] PATIENT: Uh, no, not not really, no.\n[10:30] DOCTOR: OK. And has he had any muscle aches or uh, or joint pains that that he's mentioned?\n[10:45] PATIENT: No, no he hasn't mentioned anything like that.\n[11:00] DOCTOR: OK, and he's had a fever, has he had the chills?\n[11:15] PATIENT: Yeah, so that's that's what I, that prompted me to take his temperature yesterday around the afternoon, like we had to wrap him up with like more than two blankets and he was still feeling cold so, yeah, definitely was having chills.\n[11:30] DOCTOR: OK, and and has there been any concerns with his growth, either weight or or height?\n[11:45] PATIENT: So he's been going along the charts pretty well, like no concerns during his growth. Otherwise, he's a very healthy child.\n[12:00] DOCTOR: OK, so no medical diagnosis previously?\n[12:15] PATIENT: No nothing like that.\n[12:30] DOCTOR: OK, and uh, yeah, is he meeting all developmental milestones OK? Like gross motor, fine motor, doing well at school?\n[12:45] PATIENT: Yeah yeah, he didn't have any issues with walking, running, he's pretty social with all his peers. He's doing well at school, no complaints.\n[13:00] DOCTOR: OK, excellent and you know, no medical conditions, but has there been any reasons for being in the hospital before?\n[13:15] PATIENT: No, not at all. We, yeah, we haven't really gone to the doctor with him after like all those vaccinations that he had to do, so he's been good.\n[13:30] DOCTOR: OK, great, so immunizations are up to date?\n[13:45] PATIENT: Yeah, immunizations all up to date.\n[14:00] DOCTOR: Excellent and does he have any allergies?\n[14:15] PATIENT: Uh, no, uh, not not anything that we know of yet.\n[14:30] DOCTOR: OK, and I just wanted to ask about his birth history. Were there any complications throughout throughout pregnancy, either mom being sick or having any any issues herself?\n[14:45] PATIENT: No, so it was a normal vaginal delivery, uh no issues, uh he was born at 39 weeks, didn't need to stay in the hospital at all. Yeah, it was a pretty smooth pregnancy compared to some of the other ones.\n[15:00] DOCTOR: OK, oh, that's that's great. Do you remember the Apgar score or his birth weight?\n[15:15] PATIENT: Uhm, I don't exactly remember his Apgar scores, I think they were like just just they they were normal like there was nothing concerning about them, and birth weight I think he was probably around 3 kilograms.\n[15:30] DOCTOR: OK, great so, yeah healthy birth weight at term it sounds like. And so, healthy, does he take any medications regularly?\n[15:45] PATIENT: No, no medications.\n[16:00] DOCTOR: OK, and could you tell me about who is living with currently and then who's looking after him?\n[16:15] PATIENT: Yeah, so he's living with myself, his mother and, and then my husband, and he has two older siblings. One is a boy, seven years old, and another one like a 10 year old girl.\n[16:30] DOCTOR: OK, and has anybody in the house been sick?\n[16:45] PATIENT: Uh no, no one has been sick recently, however he does, they did let them go back into school at one point, so I don't know, I don't know, so that was just last week, so I don't know if he caught something from school.\n[17:00] DOCTOR: OK, but nobody that you know that's had similar symptoms?\n[17:15] PATIENT: No.\n[17:30] DOCTOR: OK, and have you traveled anywhere?\n[17:45] PATIENT: No, we've been, we've been at home.\n[18:00] DOCTOR: OK, uh, and then you know recent changes to anything in the diet you've mentioned?\n[18:15] PATIENT: No, that's been fine.\n[18:30] DOCTOR: OK, and any concerns for safety in the home, whether physical or or verbal abuse?\n[18:45] PATIENT: No, no concerns like that.\n[19:00] DOCTOR: OK, yeah a question that we have to ask everybody and is there any smoking in the home?\n[19:15] PATIENT: No, no, we don't smoke.\n[19:30] DOCTOR: OK, great then, are there any pets?\n[19:45] PATIENT: Nope, no pets.\n[20:00] DOCTOR: Alright, and uh and in the family, has there been anybody who's had any heart problems or any lung problems?\n[20:15] PATIENT: Uh, we are getting the 10 year old tested for just uh, asthma.\n[20:30] DOCTOR: OK.\n[20:45] PATIENT: But nothing diagnosed yet, no.\n[21:00] DOCTOR: OK, anybody in the family have any congenital diseases or any genetic diseases?\n[21:15] PATIENT: No, not that I know of.\n[21:30] DOCTOR: OK, uh, anybody in the family have celiac disease or Crohn's disease?\n[21:45] PATIENT: No, no, uh no history of any kind of bowel diseases.\n[22:00] DOCTOR: OK, so I think that's, uh, everything I want I wanted to ask about today. Uh, was there anything that maybe I I forgot to ask or or that I missed that you wanted to tell me?\n[22:15] PATIENT: Uh, yeah, no it's, it's also just like I was just reading up like, can it be like I don't know if it can be COVID as well, if it can, it can do this as well?\n[22:30] DOCTOR: It is possible. Uh, it's uh, especially with nonspecific symptoms, COVID can cause a variety of different presentations, and there for some people it can be more gastrointestinal issues like like your son has with vomiting and diarrhea and belly pain. But it also could be well, uh, another type of gastrointestinal virus itself or or a viral illness like the common cold, which can also end up causing symptoms like this as well. So we will, I will go ahead and do a COVID test today, along with some other blood work. And maybe increase the hydration as well, because it sounds like he has lost a lot of fluid over these last couple of days. So we will check electrolytes and go from there on the test results.\n[22:45] PATIENT: Alright, sounds good. Thank you so much.''', soap_note={'subjective': 'Reports mild sore throat and nasal congestion for 2 days. No fever.', 'objective': 'Telehealth. Patient appears well hydrated, no respiratory distress.', 'assessment': 'Viral upper respiratory infection.', 'plan': 'Supportive care: rest, hydration, OTC lozenges. Return if fever develops or symptoms worsen after 10 days.'}, icd_codes=[{'code': 'J06.9', 'description': 'Acute upper respiratory infection, unspecified', 'version': 10}], prescription=[], patient_summary='Evaluated for mild cold symptoms. Advised supportive care.', extraction_data={}, emr_data={'vitals': {'blood_pressure': '128/82', 'heart_rate': '76', 'temperature': '98.4°F', 'spo2': '98%'}, 'chief_complaint': 'Minor illness', 'assessment': 'Viral upper respiratory infection.', 'plan': 'Supportive care: rest, hydration, OTC lozenges. Return if fever develops or symptoms worsen after 10 days.'}, status='confirmed', consultation_date=now - timedelta(days=40, hours=-9)))
